      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A 3,700‑acre tobacco plantation owned by the Riley family, a slave‑owning lineage, once covered much of what is now North Bethesda.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Bethesda%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The WMATA North Bethesda metro station, formerly White Flint, sits on the Red Line and was named after the nearby White Flint Mall that was later demolished.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Bethesda%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Music Center at Strathmore, a Neo‑Georgian mansion turned concert hall, is a cultural landmark in North Bethesda.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Bethesda%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
